  {
    "episode_id": 74434,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "I think it's really simple to do that. You go and you talk to them. You have the kind of dialogues that you do. We're going to be in a better position because number one, I believe, we'll have a better party infrastructure. in two years. Number two, I've got the ability to move around the state now. Most of the candidates that we've seen didn't have the money. They didn't have the ability to go out as early as we did. I will be in much more of a known commodity than our statewide candidates were this time. When we had some great Democratic candidates, this time they just could not get out there as much and they didn't have the funds. We will have the money. We will be able to raise the money. to make this race the kind of race we want to, and I will be out there all the time. I've never quit campaigning, but also my Senate duties take me to all four corners of the state of Alabama, and people see us, and they will be able to talk to us. and touch us and understand who I am and what I'm trying to do for the state. So I think there's a big difference. Despite the fact that there were so many, I don't think it was over 60%, but it was more than a majority that voted straight ticket Republican, but at the same time, we'll have more people voting. There'll be more in the presidential race, and historically, people will tell you that they will, those people, those extra folks that will come out, will be splitting their ballots.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
