  {
    "episode_id": 74434,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "chair and the vice chair are also members of the DNC. And so there's been two challenges filed to challenge that election and also to invalidate everything else that was done. I don't know where that will land. I know it will be heard early next year by the DNC, but it's a very serious challenge. And I think that if we can, if that challenge is even remotely successful, there will be some changes. But I think you're also seeing just a momentum from the grassroots level. In the last couple of years, it's just being a quiet disgruntled, but now people really understand and see that we have got to have a competitive two-party system. in order for this state to really progress. That doesn't mean that Democrats have to dominate. It doesn't necessarily mean Republicans do either. We just kind of have a dialogue and we've got to be competitive. And we are working to do some things to try to make sure that in the coming two years we will have a change of leadership that will be able to raise some money, develop the infrastructure to develop the social media strategy that's been non-existent. to help recruit candidates, all of which we've not seen in the last couple of election cycles. But I really believe, as I'm sitting here today, that you're going to see that change in the real near future.",
