  {
    "episode_id": 74434,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "During that time, especially after I announced my vote, we had phone calls that were just ugly, just downright ugly. I mean, phone calls where people said that they hoped that the young girl answering the phone might get sexually assaulted one day, not threatening phone calls, just ugly tribal phone calls that you really hoped in America that we wouldn't. I have and so we we watch that and those you know look I have been a lawyer for 40 years and Part of my job is as a trial lawyer is evaluating credibility I Also evaluated the credibility of those two witnesses, and I think she was very credible I believe something did happen and the fact that he so vehemently on just crazy ways didn't deny this and the way that he did that. I just felt like the credibility choice was in her favor. But again, at the end of the day, that was just one part of this that went into my thinking. The other part was really a lot about his temperament and the fact that I did not think he showed the independence. There was so much controversy surrounding this guy. All he had to do was do a couple things. Number one, say, look, I want this committee to have access, and I want the senators to have access to all of my records, not just a select group that a lawyer for him had screened. I want them to have access to as much as they do it, even if it means delaying this vote a couple of months, because there was no reason not to delay the vote. Supreme Court's operated with eight people many times in the past. And the second thing, when the allegations were made, he often said, I want to have a hearing, but he also should have said, I want to see an independent investigation to try to figure out as best you can, which would have been difficult, by the way. But let's put this on ice for a little bit until we let the FBI do an independent investigation. But no, he adopted Senator McConnell's way. We're just going to plow through this. This is a Supreme Court nomination that's going to be there probably for 20 or 30 years. You don't plow through something like that. You do it deliberately. And that's what I think the people of Alabama elected me for, not just a rubber stamp somebody or to do it or to be a knee-jerk in opposition to somebody or to rubber stamp them. I wanted to do the due diligence and I don't really believe I had an opportunity to do that.",
