    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "no, not really. Obviously, I had to live through that campaign. But if you remember, I kind of stayed away from all that in that campaign. I stated, matter of factly, that I believe the women who came forward I thought it was something that folks should consider, but then I moved on back to the issues that I thought people should take into account when they're electing a United States senator. So it did not. I think more important for me, it was my background as a lawyer, my background with Senator Heflin on the Senate Judiciary Committee and trying to know that my basic philosophy about nominations for cabinet level and administration positions is that the president should have his own team. But, and that frankly, on judicial nominations, the needle kind of is in his direction simply because I expect the president to nominate judges who reflect his basic judicial philosophy. But at the same time, the Constitution gives the Senate an independent role in determining that, a dual role with the president. So, I wanted to exercise that independence. But I looked at this separate. I wish people could see the binders and binders of information that we reviewed with his record, his judicial opinions, his speeches, his writings. You name it. We knew it a lot, but there was still so much more that we didn't know. And again, there are just some things in there. I was very uncomfortable with some of the answers that he gave in the very beginning and the first hearing. I was uncomfortable with a lot of the answers based on some documents that I saw.",
