    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 60,
    "turn_text": "Remarkable change. And really not for the better in a couple of ways. Number one, The Senate moves at a different pace right now and by that I mean there's so much that is not what they call regular order. Regular order means that you're trying to do the budget process on a rolling basis. Now Senator Shelby and Senator Leahy on appropriations are trying to get us back to that. And they've done an incredible job this year trying to get our budgets done on time. In the last 15 or 20 years, it's been kicking the can down the road. And we're still doing it a little bit this year, but it's mainly because of the house. And they were a little slow to catch up to us. I think that there's a couple of differences though. People in the Senate now, very few of them actually live in Washington. You've either been there a long, long time or you're on the West Coast or Hawaii or somewhere and you don't get home as much. People usually get out of there on Thursdays because it is so difficult these days. There are so many demands for fundraising for the next real act, even if it's five years down the road or folks at home wanting your time. And so with people not up there together as much on the weekends and in social settings and with their families, they just don't get to know each other as well. there are more caucus meetings, Democrats meet with Democrats and Republicans meet with Republicans, and there's not as much meeting together and doing some things. There's a fair amount that goes on with that, but not as much as there was when Judge Heflin was in the Senate, at least when I was there in the late 70s. And I think that that really has hurt the Senate in the sense of trying to fashion a lot more bipartisanship. You know, senators campaign against each other. You never saw that happen in those days. They just let the people in their state handle it now. You got these super PACs and everybody that Republicans have going against incumbent Democrats have going against incumbent Republicans. That just seems a little unseemly to me when you're trying to build some bipartisanship. It's one thing to do it on an open seat. It's another to do it with a colleague. And so I think the collegiality is still there, but it's just not as deep as it was",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The current state of the Senate represents a remarkable change.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "This change is not positive in several respects.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "A 'remarkable change' implies significant transformation in institutional behavior.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Positive institutional outcomes depend on bipartisan cooperation and personal relationships among senators.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Collegiality among senators is necessary for effective governance.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The decline in informal interactions between senators undermines trust and collaboration.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Bipartisan legislation requires meaningful interpersonal connections beyond formal party structures.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 74434,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "there. Yeah, I mean, look, in maintenance state, there's a lot more bipartisanship that goes on than the US Senate than people see. Because of C-SPAN, you're seeing dueling press conferences and you're seeing, you know, speeches that are more political speeches than anything else on C-SPAN that are really speeches to an empty chamber, but when it's dealing with committee work and legislation, there's a lot more effort to bring people in. I think I've co-sponsored 110 or so pieces of legislation and a hundred of them are bipartisan. I've done about nine or ten of my own where I'm an original sponsor and at least seven or eight of those are with a Republican colleague. So there's a lot more work that goes on behind the scenes that people don't know of, both at the staff level, as well as at the legislative level.",
