  {
    "episode_id": 74434,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74434.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 91,
    "turn_text": "A wall, new technology in the sense of drones, more border guards, more immigration judges, everything to protect the border. See, that's the president always talks about the borders, the borders, the borders, the borders and protecting the borders. Everyone agrees with that. There is no one, there is no one that wants open borders. That bill that we fashion, I was a part of, would have funded border security for up to $25 billion. That included money for a wall where a wall could be built. There's a lot of issues with that, but it also had increased technology. It did all the things that his Homeland Security told us they wanted. At the end of the day, though, the hardliners in the administration decided they wanted more. They wanted to change the legal immigration, the visas, the diversity visas, the seasonal worker visas, what they called the chain migration and other people called family reunification. The same program that Melania Trump's parents came to this country and they wanted to change all of that. And so the border security went by the wayside. And that bill got defeated by Republicans by about three or four votes. And that was unfortunate. So I think right now what I would really like to see initially is I would like to see that bill be brought back. And we're looking at maybe doing that. that bill is a start and that would fund the wall to an extent but it would be again border security not internal enforcement that's a separate budgetary item but fund border security also do get the dreamers taken care of then we can deal with any other comprehensive immigration down the road now will say this i think there may be some appetite for bringing back what they all called the old gang of eight bill that went down in flames after a couple of people decided that were sponsors decided to run for president and it all of a sudden didn't look to be as good a political idea for them but it's a it was a really good bill that would have funded border security to forty billion dollars So, if that bill had passed, and it did pass the Senate, we, you know, we might not be having the same kind of discussions. So, on the front end, at least DACA and border security, we can look at the others because everyone that you talk to, every policy advisor, every economist will tell you we need a healthy legal immigration system. Right now, I was just up in center Alabama talking about the farming industry. and how they can't get good workers. How people right now, when we've got low unemployment, people are dying to get folks to come in there, both as seasonal workers, as well as long-term workers. And we could do that, I think, with a good, with some just minor tweaks to the immigration packages, and at the same time do our real work with DACA and the Dreamers, as well as Board of Security.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
